³1ip³2217.247.142.155³2ts³21095194185³2version³21³2name³2text_default³2data³2summary³3*³3text³3= Natural Law =

:<b>"The literature of natural law is complex, copious, and monthly growing vaster."</b>
::<b>&#151;Russell Kirk, The Heritage Foundation</b>

<b>What is natural law?</b>

This is a conception of ethics. 'Natural' means from nature as in the physical universe. 'Law' means principle or order as in the Law of Physics. Taken together natural law is the view that there are laws of ethics based on physical reality identifiable by human reason. If existent or arguably relevant, this allows an absolute form of ethics standing in contrast to relativist ethics based on societal norms or the nihilist's lack of ethical standards.

Historically and today among religionists God plays the cental role. In that God created nature, all natural law is God's law and thus it becomes a conception of morals. And in that conception, above natural law is devine law. The nature and extent of devine law is still less than well understood.

A side issue is the relationship between natural law and human law. Natural law can be either physically based or God based, and the human law can be any of the many found in law theory which itself "is complex, copious, and monthly growing vaster".

For many in <nowiki>AnCap</nowiki>, physical natural law is the interest, and the side issue shrinks to human law limited by natural law. But also note that the current discussion widens the basis of physical law beyond "atoms and energy" to include "laws of thought" such as logic. That widening is generally not accepted by today's academia.


<b>A continuation on all aspects can be found in:</b>

:The Case For and Against Natural Law by Russell Kirk (Heritage Foundation).
::http://www.townhall.com/hall_of_fame/kirk/kirk469.html

<b>Some history of the problem closer to the <nowiki>AnCap</nowiki> tradition is:</b>

:The Non-Absurdity of Natural Law by Wendy McElroy http://www.zetetics.com/mac/natlaw.htm

<b>And a variety from different viewpoints:</b>

:Natural Law [Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy]. http://www.iep.utm.edu/n/natlaw.htm

:The Natural Law Tradition in Ethics.  Key Features of Natural Law Theories.
::http://plato.stanford.edu/entries/natural-law-ethics/

:What is Natural Law? Examination of American jurisprudence through natural law theory.
::http://www.jurisdictionary.com/Essays/natural_law.htm

:<i>The Natural Law, a study in legal and social history and philosophy</i> by Heinrich A. Rommen. From Liberty Fund, 'Ama-gi'. 
::http://www.friesian.com/rommen.htm (Good site for libertarian & philosophy)

&nbsp;

